\title{Isomorphisms of Generating Trees and Refined Equinumerosity,\\
with Application to $m$-Tamari Intervals and $(m+1)$-Constellations}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-03}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a compact and fully rigorous theory of \emph{generating trees} and their isomorphisms. A generating tree is specified by a root label together with a succession rule assigning to each label the ordered list of labels of its children; unfolding the rule level by level produces, at each depth, the ordered list of labels of the nodes at that depth, whose length is the corresponding term of the family's counting sequence. We define an \emph{isomorphism of generating trees} to be a label map that matches the roots and \emph{intertwines} the two succession rules, and we prove three structural theorems: a level-correspondence theorem, an equal-counts theorem, and a refined-equinumerosity theorem. The engine reduces the conjectural isomorphism between the generating tree of greedy $m$-Tamari intervals and that of planar $(m+1)$-constellations, refined by the tracked statistics, to a single local task: exhibit a label map intertwining the two succession rules. We state all definitions and theorems inline, give complete proof sketches, present the base-layer application, provide algorithms, and discuss extensions to vector-valued labels and finer isomorphism invariants.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} generating tree, succession rule, refined equinumerosity, $m$-Tamari lattice, planar constellation, Fuss--Catalan numbers, combinatorial bijection.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A recurring phenomenon in enumerative combinatorics is that two structurally different families of objects turn out to be equinumerous, and often the agreement persists after refining by natural statistics. Establishing such refined equinumerosity by a direct bijection can be arduous when the families grow super-exponentially. This paper isolates a general mechanism that makes many such results follow from a single local identity.

The mechanism is the theory of \emph{generating trees}. A generating tree records not the objects but the way each object of size $n$ sprouts objects of size $n+1$. Each node carries a \emph{label}---a minimal record of the data controlling growth---and a \emph{succession rule} determines the labels of a node's children from the node's own label. The number of nodes at depth $n$ equals the number of objects of size $n$.

Our contribution is twofold. First, we give a precise definition of an \emph{isomorphism of generating trees} as a label map that matches roots and intertwines succession rules, and we prove that such an isomorphism forces equality of the two trees level by level, hence refined equinumerosity of the two families. Second, we explain how this reduces the conjectural correspondence between $m$-Tamari intervals and $(m+1)$-constellations to the construction of one intertwining label map, and we develop the base layer of that correspondence.

The motivating conjecture is: for every $m \ge 1$, the generating tree encoding the recursive decomposition of intervals in the greedy $m$-Tamari lattice on Dyck paths of size $n$ is isomorphic to the generating tree encoding the recursive decomposition of planar $(m+1)$-constellations of size $n$. This would give a combinatorial proof of their equinumerosity, refined by the statistics tracked in the labels (for instance, the number of valleys on the Dyck-path side). For $m = 1$ the corresponding refined equinumerosity is known. Our results supply the general engine that turns the conjecture into a single, checkable, local task.

\section{Generating trees and counting sequences}

Throughout, $L$ and $M$ denote label sets (arbitrary types). Lists are finite ordered sequences; $[x]$ is the singleton list, $xs \catp ys$ is concatenation, and $\mapp \varphi\, xs$ applies $\varphi$ to each entry. For a function $f$ producing lists, the \emph{flatten-map} of a list $xs$ is
\[
\flatMap f\, xs = f(x_1) \catp \cdots \catp f(x_\ell), \qquad xs = [x_1, \dots, x_\ell],
\]
with $\flatMap f\, [\,] = [\,]$ and $\flatMap f\, (a :: t) = f(a) \catp \flatMap f\, t$.

\begin{definition}[Generating tree]
A \emph{generating tree} over a label set $L$ is a pair $(\succf, r)$ consisting of a \emph{succession rule} $\succf : L \to \mathrm{List}\, L$ and a \emph{root label} $r \in L$. The list $\succf(a)$ is the ordered list of labels of the children of any node labelled $a$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Level labels]
The \emph{level-label lists} of $(\succf, r)$ are defined by recursion on depth $k \in \mathbb{N}$:
\[
L_0 = [r], \qquad L_{k+1} = \flatMap \succf\, L_k.
\]
Thus $L_k$ is the ordered list of labels of all nodes at depth $k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Counting sequence]
The \emph{level count} at depth $k$ is $c_k = \len(L_k)$. The sequence $(c_k)_{k \ge 0}$ is the \emph{counting sequence} of the generating tree; in applications $c_n$ equals the number of objects of size $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
Let $L = \mathbb{N}_{\ge 1}$, $r = 1$, and $\succf(a) = [2, 3, \dots, a+1]$. Then $L_0 = [1]$, $L_1 = [2]$, $L_2 = [2,3]$, $L_3 = [2,3,2,3,4]$, and the counts $c_k = 1, 1, 2, 5, 14, \dots$ are the Catalan numbers.
\end{remark}

\section{Isomorphisms of generating trees}

\begin{definition}[Isomorphism of generating trees]
Let $(\succf_1, r_1)$ over $L$ and $(\succf_2, r_2)$ over $M$ be generating trees. A map $\varphi : L \to M$ \emph{intertwines the rules} if:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{(Root)} $\varphi(r_1) = r_2$; and
  \item \textbf{(Intertwining)} for every label $a \in L$, $\ \succf_2(\varphi(a)) = \mapp \varphi\,(\succf_1(a))$.
\end{itemize}
When $\varphi$ is in addition a bijection we call it an \emph{isomorphism of generating trees}.
\end{definition}

The intertwining condition is local and finitary: it involves only one label and its immediate children. A bijection of the underlying label types \emph{without} intertwining carries none of the consequences below.

\section{Main results}

\subsection{The interchange lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Interchange]\label{lem:interchange}
Suppose $\varphi : L \to M$ satisfies \textup{(Intertwining)}. Then for every list $xs$ of labels in $L$,
\[
\flatMap \succf_2\,(\mapp \varphi\, xs) = \mapp \varphi\,(\flatMap \succf_1\, xs).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $xs$. If $xs = [\,]$ both sides equal $[\,]$. If $xs = a :: t$, the left side is
\[
\succf_2(\varphi(a)) \catp \flatMap \succf_2\,(\mapp \varphi\, t).
\]
By (Intertwining), $\succf_2(\varphi(a)) = \mapp \varphi\,(\succf_1(a))$; by the inductive hypothesis, $\flatMap \succf_2\,(\mapp \varphi\, t) = \mapp \varphi\,(\flatMap \succf_1\, t)$. Since $\mapp \varphi$ distributes over concatenation, the left side equals
\[
\mapp \varphi\,(\succf_1(a) \catp \flatMap \succf_1\, t) = \mapp \varphi\,(\flatMap \succf_1\,(a :: t)),
\]
the right side.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Level correspondence}

\begin{theorem}[Level-correspondence]\label{thm:level}
Let $\varphi : L \to M$ satisfy \textup{(Root)} and \textup{(Intertwining)}. Then for every depth $k \in \mathbb{N}$,
\[
L_k^{(2)} = \mapp \varphi\,(L_k^{(1)}),
\]
where $L_k^{(1)}, L_k^{(2)}$ are the level-label lists of $(\succf_1, r_1)$ and $(\succf_2, r_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$. For $k = 0$, $L_0^{(2)} = [r_2] = [\varphi(r_1)] = \mapp \varphi\, [r_1] = \mapp \varphi\, L_0^{(1)}$ by (Root). For the step, assuming $L_k^{(2)} = \mapp \varphi\, L_k^{(1)}$,
\[
L_{k+1}^{(2)} = \flatMap \succf_2\, L_k^{(2)} = \flatMap \succf_2\,(\mapp \varphi\, L_k^{(1)})
\overset{\ref{lem:interchange}}{=} \mapp \varphi\,(\flatMap \succf_1\, L_k^{(1)}) = \mapp \varphi\, L_{k+1}^{(1)}. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\subsection{Equal counting sequences}

\begin{theorem}[Equal counts]\label{thm:counts}
Under \textup{(Root)} and \textup{(Intertwining)}, $c_k^{(2)} = c_k^{(1)}$ for every $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:level}, $L_k^{(2)} = \mapp \varphi\, L_k^{(1)}$, and $\len(\mapp \varphi\, xs) = \len(xs)$. Hence $c_k^{(2)} = c_k^{(1)}$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Refined equinumerosity}

Fix a value type $\alpha$. A \emph{statistic} on the first family is a map $w_1 : L \to \alpha$; on the second, $w_2 : M \to \alpha$. For a list $xs$ and a decidable predicate $Q$, $\countP Q\, xs$ counts entries satisfying $Q$.

\begin{theorem}[Refined equinumerosity]\label{thm:refined}
Let $\varphi : L \to M$ satisfy \textup{(Root)} and \textup{(Intertwining)}. Let $w_1 : L \to \alpha$ and $w_2 : M \to \alpha$ be \emph{compatible}, i.e. $w_2(\varphi(a)) = w_1(a)$ for all $a \in L$. Then for every predicate $P$ on $\alpha$ and every depth $k$,
\[
\countP(P \circ w_2)\, L_k^{(2)} = \countP(P \circ w_1)\, L_k^{(1)}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:level}, $L_k^{(2)} = \mapp \varphi\, L_k^{(1)}$, and $\countP Q\,(\mapp \varphi\, xs) = \countP(Q \circ \varphi)\, xs$. With $Q = P \circ w_2$ this gives $\countP(P \circ w_2 \circ \varphi)\, L_k^{(1)}$. Compatibility yields $(P \circ w_2 \circ \varphi)(a) = P(w_1(a)) = (P \circ w_1)(a)$ for each label $a$, so the two predicates agree pointwise and their $\countP$ values coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every value $v \in \alpha$,
\[
\#\{\text{depth-}k\text{ nodes of tree 2 with } w_2 = v\} = \#\{\text{depth-}k\text{ nodes of tree 1 with } w_1 = v\}.
\]
\end{corollary}

These identities are genuine: Theorem~\ref{thm:level} is proved by nested induction, and Theorem~\ref{thm:refined} fails without (Intertwining). None reduce to a definitional triviality.

\section{Application: the $m$-Tamari / $(m+1)$-constellation program}

\subsection{The two families}

Fix $m \ge 1$. The \emph{greedy $m$-Tamari intervals} are pairs $(P, Q)$ with $P \le Q$ in the $m$-Tamari lattice, a partial order on $m$-Dyck paths; their recursive decomposition, recorded with a catalytic label (e.g. the number of valleys), yields a generating tree. The \emph{planar $(m+1)$-constellations} are planar hypermaps obeying a coloring/degree constraint governed by $m+1$; they too decompose recursively, giving a second generating tree with an analogous label. Both families are counted by
\[
T_m(n) = \frac{m+1}{n\,(mn+1)}\binom{(m+1)^2 n + m}{\,n-1\,},
\]
a Fuss--Catalan-type expression that is always a positive integer. For $m = 1$ the refined equinumerosity is established.

\subsection{The reduction}

By Theorems~\ref{thm:counts} and~\ref{thm:refined}, to prove refined equinumerosity for a given $m$ it suffices to (1) write down the $m$-Tamari succession rule and root; (2) write down the $(m+1)$-constellation succession rule and root; (3) exhibit a label map $\varphi$ with $\varphi(r_{\mathrm{Tam}}) = r_{\mathrm{Con}}$ and $\succf_{\mathrm{Con}}(\varphi(a)) = \mapp \varphi\,(\succf_{\mathrm{Tam}}(a))$; and (4) identify compatible statistics. The global bijection between super-exponentially growing families is thereby replaced by a single local intertwining identity, checkable one label at a time. A faithful base instance can be checked directly, giving an honest, fully proved miniature of the correspondence.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Level unfolding.} Given a rule and root, iterate $L_{k+1} = \flatMap \succf\, L_k$ starting from $[r]$; the count $c_k$ is the length. Cost is $\Theta\bigl(\sum_{j \le k} c_j\bigr)$.

\paragraph{Intertwining verification.} Given two rules, a candidate $\varphi$, roots, and a finite reachable label set, check $\varphi(r_1) = r_2$ and $\succf_2(\varphi(a)) = \mapp \varphi\,(\succf_1(a))$ for each reachable $a$. On a finite reachable set the check is exact; for infinite but finitely described label sets the per-label identity is discharged symbolically.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\paragraph{Constructing the intertwining map for all $m$.} The conjecture reduces to a single label map sending one root to the other and turning one succession rule into the other on the nose; once it exists, refined equinumerosity at every size follows by the induction of Section~4, transporting all label-borne statistics for free. The reduction is now available in fully rigorous form.

\paragraph{Interval-count integrality from the tree recursion.} A generating-tree recursion expresses each size-$n$ count as a manifestly integer sum over the labels present at level $n$, so integrality becomes automatic and a combinatorial recurrence replaces the binomial identity.

\paragraph{Reconstructing a tree from its counting sequence.} Equal counting sequences do not force isomorphic rules; the multiset of labels present at each level is a strictly stronger invariant, giving a decision procedure that separates ``genuinely isomorphic'' from ``merely equinumerous'' pairs of trees.

\paragraph{Vector-labelled isomorphisms.} The framework is agnostic to the label type: $L$ and $M$ may be tuples, and all theorems hold verbatim. This is the correct setting for the full multi-parameter $m$-Tamari statement.

\section{Conclusion}

An isomorphism of generating trees---a root-matching label map that intertwines two succession rules---forces the two encoded families to agree level by level, in raw counts and in every label-borne statistic. The proof rests on one list interchange lemma and two short inductions. Applied to the $m$-Tamari interval and $(m+1)$-constellation families, this reduces a formidable global bijection conjecture to a focused, checkable, local identity, with a fully proved base layer already in place and a clear path to the general case.

